# Your actual financial data. Never commit this.
#
# The ledger lives in ~/.finance-hub/, outside any checkout — the strongest
# protection is that it is not in the working tree at all. These entries are
# the net under that: a copy someone drops in to look at, a scratch book
# pointed here with FINANCE_DB, anything a crash leaves behind.
data/
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Every CSV, not just the export filenames. A bank statement saved into the
# repo to try something out is the likeliest way real transactions get staged,
# and it arrives named stmt.csv or eStmt_2025-04-01_1234.csv, matching nothing
# specific.
*.csv

# The one exception, and it is a directory rather than a pattern on purpose: a
# statement you just downloaded lands in ~/Downloads, never in test/fixtures/.
# Everything in there is invented content in a real bank's shape — see its
# README. githooks/pre-commit carries the same exception, and api.test.js
# fails if a .csv in there is not read by a test, so one dropped in and
# forgotten does not sit there quietly.
!test/fixtures/*.csv

# Statements that arrive as a PDF: a plan's quarterly statement, a statement page
# saved with "Save as PDF". The same wholesale block and the same single
# exception, for the same reason — and the same test in api.test.js fails on a
# .pdf in test/fixtures/ that nothing reads.
*.pdf
!test/fixtures/*.pdf

# Local backups and exports
*.json.bak
finance_backup*.json

# Phase 2 broker credentials (Shioaji certificates, IBKR Flex tokens). Nothing
# reads these yet; the entry exists so the first one to arrive is already
# ignored rather than ignored a commit too late.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pfx
*.p12

.DS_Store
node_modules/

# What `scripts/pack-demo.js` writes. Every file in there is a copy of one
# that is already committed, so committing the output would be two copies of
# the frontend in one repo — and the one nobody edits is the one that gets
# served. Rebuild it instead; it takes milliseconds.
dist/
